The approximate half of math.h - sin, exp, log, pow, fma and the rest -
from musl, a C library for Linux, as it is upstream, with TheTaaJ's
changes on top (see the history of this directory).

  from     https://musl.libc.org
  release  1.2.5 (musl-1.2.5.tar.gz,
           sha256 a9a118bbe84d8764da0ea0d28b3ab3fae8477fc7e4085d90102b8596fc7c75e4)

musl because its functions are small, in plain C, each in a file of its
own, within an ulp of the exact answer (two for the hyperbolic ones),
and under the MIT licence (COPYRIGHT).

Here are only the double functions LLVM and libc++ call, and what they
call in turn, all from musl's src/math:

  __math_oflow __math_uflow __math_xflow __math_invalid __math_divzero
  __rem_pio2_large __rem_pio2 __sin __cos __tan sin cos tan asin acos
  atan atan2 __expo2 expm1 exp_data exp exp2 sinh cosh tanh log_data log
  log2_data log2 log10 log1p pow_data pow cbrt asinh acosh atanh fma

and the four tables' headers (exp_data.h, log_data.h, log2_data.h,
pow_data.h).

TheTaaJ's own, not musl's:

  libm.h    what musl's src/internal/libm.h gives these files (its
            macros, the bit-twiddling helpers, the private functions'
            declarations), and nothing else
  floats.c  the float functions (sinf, powf, ...) as the double ones
            rounded once more, instead of musl's own float files

TheTaaJ's changes to musl's files:

  acosh.c          for x below 1 the answer is NaN; musl gave a number
                   for x below -2 (journal entry 074)
  *_data.h         include libm.h, for `hidden`, instead of <features.h>
  fma.c            includes libm.h, for a_clz_64, instead of atomic.h

Tested on the host against glibc's long double functions by
libc/c/libm_test.c (`make libc-test`).
